tion of  the emergencies act is an unnecessary  and disproportionate measure that can  violate civil liberties invades  provincial jurisdiction and creates a  very dangerous but precedent for the  future and it's not necessary  weeks of protests and counter protests  have exposed deep divisions in this  country particularly over pandemic  public health measures in alberta where  the couts border crossing was blocked by  demonstrators the edmonton police chief  issued a plea to the public chris chacon  explains  a divided community  can be a dangerous community edmonton  police chief dale mcphee has been  calling for calm he has described the  political divisions highlighted by covet  19 policy as a fire i'm asking and  imploring political leaders  community members  everyone across the spectrum to help us  put this fire out but the fires continue  to rage on parliament hill divisions are  clear in edmonton on wednesday a staff  member at a liberal mp's edmonton office  was attacked the person who did show up  was a self-declared unvaccinated person  who was there to protest  and  and then the assault took place rhetoric  from leaders has been just as heated  this response comes from a question from  a jewish mp conservative party members  can stand with people who wave swastikas  they can stand with people who wave the  confederate  the house speaker admonished the prime  minister for that remark later former  mayors criticized premier jason kenney  for fanning the flames of divisiveness  after a tweet on pipeline protests and  david suzuki university of alberta  sociologist amy kaler says it has been a  long time since we've experienced this  kind of a divide the first thing that  comes to mind maybe because it's almost  exactly 100 years ago is if you look at  the descent and divergent opinions and  views around  the first world war conscription the  good news here kaler says this time the  divisions might not be as deep we hear  from those most upset but kaler says  most people aren't like that the bad  news all that noise could make it harder  to find common ground  seeing these things almost gives a sort  of permission effect for them to  continue because yeah it's real i saw  somebody can do it we've done it so you  know let's keep doing it and it'll take  a while i think for that to  die down meaning says kaler some views  need to change and like chief mcphee  says so too does the rhetoric chris  chicon global news  still ahead survival at sea rescuers  find a man alive after days drifting  aboard a burning ferry  you're watching global national  we'll take a look at this startling site  in chile thousands of dead sardines and  anchovies washed up on a beach  environmental officials are now  investigating the cause but locals say  that low oxygen levels in deeper waters  sometimes force the fish to swim closer  to the shore in search of nutrients  a plane chartered by the spanish  government landed in newfoundland today  to repatriate the survivors and some of  the victims of a shipwreck last week the  aircraft touched down in saint john's  and is expected to return three sailors  to spain tomorrow along with five of the  ten victims the remaining bodies will be  repatriated to peru eleven crew members  of that commercial fishing boat are  still missing  and a survivor has been pulled from a  burning ferry off the coast of greece  the bella russian man was reported  missing and presumed dead after the boat  caught fire three days ago he was found  in the stern of the italian-owned euro  ferry olympia the body of another  passenger was also recovered 10 people  are still unaccounted for an  investigation into the cause of that  fire is now underway  game's over coming up the closing  ceremony and reflections from the  olympics in beijing  [Music]  it was a winter olympics plagued by  covet 19 fears political controversies  and yet another russian doping scandal  the beijing games themselves though led  to some great results for canadian  athletes ross lord reports  shedding a cloud of controversy the  winter games ended in elegance true  television ratings for the beijing  olympics were at rock bottom levels but  many things that could have gone wrong  didn't it was wonderful sport it was  joyous sport  uh  the feared covet outbreaks did not occur  so the organizers managed that very well  also managing very well team canada a  medal count of 26 though heavy on bronze  and light on gold that total compares  favorably with canada's count at  previous winter games and this country  has new stars like ottawa speed skater  isabel weidemann named the flag bearer  at the closing ceremonies after winning  a bronze a silver and a gold medal  another canadian athlete hockey player  marie philippe poulet continued a run of  olympic excellence team captain pulan  scored the golden goal in the final game  for the third time propelling the  canadian women to an inspiring victory  over their american arch rivals  delivering a relatively smooth olympics  allows chinese officials to boast now  that beijing is the only city to host  both the summer and winter games  some say it's a public relations ruse  for a communist regime notorious for  alleged human rights abuses it does  cover over some major  major issues that do need to be dealt  with um and i think moving forward  canada needs to step up and and and  think about those human rights issues  the next winter olympics in 2026 will be  held in the italian cities of milan and  cortina dom pezzo sports fans will be  hoping for less political strife  and that the pandemic will feel like a  distant memory  ross lord global news that is global  national for this sunday night i'm jeff  semple tonight your canada is a real  hoot it's this snowy owl near meford  we would love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching calling christy will  be at the anchor desk with you t